The coder's temporary true-race probe demonstrates that the current `lstat`-then-`server.listen(path)` sequence in `packages/music-core/session/server.ts:148-215,646-656` is not atomic: two contenders can both observe a missing path and both fulfill startup, allowing two provider/coordinator graphs to be acquired. This violates socket bind as final singleton authority and means marker bypass/races can produce duplicate owners. Replace the preflight TOCTOU design with an atomic listener-acquisition mechanism, retain exact bound-path cleanup ownership, and commit the simultaneous-race regression proving exactly one provider winner and a still-connectable endpoint.

### Critical — Selected listener-first shutdown still deadlocks on blocked coordinator work

`server.ts:665-724` still awaits connection children before closing the selected coordinator scope. Connection forwarding can wait for a blocked coordinator worker, while that worker is interrupted only by the later `closeCoordinator`. The blocked-sampling fixture still owns its coordinator outside the selected post-bind graph, so it bypasses this production cycle. Redesign the listener/coordinator lifetime and run the blocked-work regression through the real topology.

### High — Remaining startup acceptance evidence is incomplete

The package still lacks deterministic `TestClock` schedule/lifecycle coverage, exact marker release on interruption and complete-workflow spawn failure, release-diagnostic retention, 20-way `connectOrStart` convergence, and terminal skew behavior before/after marker acquisition and while waiting. The true bind-race probe must also be retained as green evidence after the atomic acquisition fix rather than removed.

